Package: preENM
Type: Package
Title: Data Preparation and Model Selection for Ecological Niche and Species
    Distribution Models
Version: 2.39.0
Authors@R:
    person("Kamil", "Konowalik", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           email = "kamil.konowalik@gmail.com")
Description: Tools for the steps around fitting ecological niche models /
    species distribution models (ENM/SDM) with any software. Data
    preparation: transparent, rule-based filtering of occurrence records
    (especially GBIF downloads), including detection of atlas grid data,
    coordinate precision, settlements, sea, range and climatic outliers, with
    optional tests from 'CoordinateCleaner'; matching of scientific names
    to GBIF, ChecklistBank or a local taxonomic backbone, with tests for
    unmatched names, synonyms of other taxa and ranks out of scope;
    coordinate uncertainty judged by the variation of the predictors within
    it, or propagated through replicate datasets with records moved within
    their uncertainty, and the resulting spread of the predictions;
    merging of presence-only, presence-absence and abundance data with
    filtering settings per data type, and export of the input of integrated
    species distribution models (for 'intSDM', 'PointedSDMs' or 'biomod2'); a check for
    niches truncated by the calibration area; delimitation of the accessible
    area (M) from a data-derived linking distance, spatially adaptive
    buffers or simulated dispersal, with room for expert additions, the
    minimal extent whose environment has stabilised, and how evenly M
    offers the conditions of the records; exact batch cropping of raster files to M; derived climate
    variables (vapour pressure deficit, potential evapotranspiration,
    moisture deficit, aridity, heat stress, summer rain, heat-rain
    synchrony, growing degree days, frost, continentality, dry season,
    growing period, Emberger and Ellenberg quotients, Kira's warmth
    and coldness indices, radiation and day length) from monthly
    climatologies of the present, the future or the past, with download
    of 'CHELSA' and 'CHELSA-TraCE21k' data for a region; selection of
    predictor variables uncorrelated in every period, scenario and region,
    ranked by transferability; sensitivity of records kept, coordinate
    uncertainty, correlation structure and the selected variables to the
    grain of the rasters; tests of whether predictors are more
    informative than spatially structured random variables, before
    modelling and inside the models; one record per raster cell; thinning of
    records in environmental space that evens out sampling density without
    truncating the niche environmentally or geographically; background
    points drawn uniformly, evenly across environmental space, by
    recording effort (e.g. GBIF effort layers) or both, or from the
    records of a target group (raw, kernel-smoothed or observer-weighted),
    with a check of how many points represent the environment of the
    calibration area; a sensitivity analysis of how thinning and background
    choices change the data design; and preparation of
    dated fossil records from 'Neotoma' with radiocarbon calibration. Model evaluation and selection: the
    simultaneous use of the area under the ROC curve (AUC) and the mean
    absolute error (MAE) proposed in Konowalik and Nosol (2021)
    <doi:10.1038/s41598-020-80062-1>, the true skill statistic and the
    continuous Boyce index (moving window or smoothed P/E curve, with
    bootstrap intervals); calibration diagnostics (reliability
    diagram, calibration slope and intercept, Brier score decomposition)
    usable as Pareto criteria; thresholds of continuous predictions under
    several rules (including the mutation point of the predicted-to-expected
    curve) and the cells on which they agree; spatial block
    and environmental cross-validation, with an audit of the folds for
    data leakage, spatial and environmental separation and spatial sorting
    bias (with a distance-matched background); ensembles of the selected
    models (median, mean, PCAm or weighted) with maps of the disagreement
    between them; partition of projection uncertainty among climate
    models, scenarios, algorithms and runs; maps of biogeographical
    ignorance from recording effort; checks of the ecological plausibility
    of response curves; range metrics for conservation assessment (extent
    of occurrence, area of occupancy, locations, patch structure) under
    several threshold rules; niche overlap between species, lineages or
    cytotypes in environmental space with equivalency and similarity tests
    and their sensitivity to methodological choices; temporal
    validation of hindcasts against dated (e.g. fossil) records; and model
    selection by conventional minimum values or Pareto optimality on
    user-chosen metrics, with bootstrap estimates of selection robustness; a
    block size from the spatial autocorrelation of the predictors; maps
    of extrapolation risk in projections; significance of the metrics
    against random locations drawn by recording effort; and the 'ODMAP'
    reporting protocol filled from the objects of the workflow.
